      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The acute impact in vivo from a selfexpanding stent on the vessel wall has not been sufficiently evaluated."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "We sought to compare acute in vivo injury on the vessel wall and the clinical impact between a self-expanding coronary stent and conventional balloon-expandable stents immediately after stent implantation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We included 40 patients (45 vessels) with stable or unstable angina who were assigned to either the self-expanding stent (vProtect Luminal Shield) group (n = 9; Group 1) or the conventional balloon-expandable stent group (n = 36; Group 2). Optical coherence tomography (OCT) was performed after stent deployment, as were qualitative and quantitative assessments of tissue prolapse, intrastent dissection, edge dissection and incomplete stent apposition."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Tissue prolapse was visible in all vessels in both groups. The corrected tissue prolapse area by stent length was larger in Group 2 than in Group 1 (0.06 +/- 0.06 vs. 0.02 +/- 0.01 mm(2); p < 0.001). Intrastent dissection was more frequently seen in Group 2 (33/36 vs. 4/9 vessels; p = 0.004) and the mean length of the dissection flap was greater in Group 2 than in Group 1 (277.6 +/- 110.0 vs. 76.9 +/- 103.7 microm; p < 0.001). Although edge dissection was not detected in Group 1, it was visible in 19/36 vessels (52.8%) in Group 2. The frequency of incomplete stent apposition was not significantly different between Group 2 and Group 1 (23/36 vs. 7/9 vessels, p = 0.7), but the mean depth of incomplete stent apposition was greater in Group 2 than in Group 1 (268.2 +/- 72.1 vs. 178.2 +/- 156.7 microm, p = 0.03)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "A selfexpanding stent was associated with less intrastent dissection and edge dissection than conventional balloon-expandable stents with OCT."
        }
      ]},
